  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Sensible weather will continue to be quite agreeable for \noutdoor activities. Aside from a ribbon of clouds moving through \nfrom north to south this evening, a return to sunny days is expected \nas high pressure slowly builds in through the weekend. Main impacts \nfor this period will be related to winds this evening on Lake \nChamplain, and then possible fire weather concerns over the weekend \nwith critically low relative humidity values likely in parts of\nthe region.\n\nA moisture-starved cold front passed through the region today, \ndetached from a ribbon of higher moisture behind it. Satellite \nimagery shows this cloud cover is tied to a corridor of higher \nrelative humidity in the 925-850 millibar layer, with the air mass \nabove that layer very dry. Hence, north of this region there are \njust some diurnally driven clouds and we can expect skies in our \nregion to trend clear overnight. \n\nA period of relatively fast pressure rises this evening is \nexpected with accelerated northerly flow through the Champlain \nValley as skies clear out again. Roughly between 7 PM and 10 PM \nwe expect the strongest northerly winds in the 20 to 30 knot \nrange on Lake Champlain with similar gusts in the eastern \nChamplain Valley. Northerly, rather than a more common \nnorthwesterly, flow will continue into Friday with a general \ndecay of wind speeds as high pressure builds in. \n\nThe lightening wind will lead to a chilly night and have leaned\ntowards colder guidance. At perennial cool spot SLK/Adirondack \nRegional Airport, a daily minimum temperature is possible (14 \ndegrees). Any passing high clouds should have little affect on \nsurface temperatures during the period. Also, as temperatures \nmoderate aloft, both Saturday and Sunday will feature the return\nof the typical cold season surface inversion with mountain \nsummits relative mild. Generally Sunday morning looks a bit less\ncold than Saturday morning as light south winds will interfere\nwith the nocturnal boundary layer.\n\nForecast soundings through the next three days show very dry air\naloft and correspondingly anomalously low precipitable water.\nWhile localized low humidity is expected tomorrow, cool\nnortherly advection doesn't favor particularly low surface\nhumidity at the surface. Dew points in the 20s are expected \nover the next couple of days, and with milder temperatures on \nSaturday reaching the upper 50s in most locations, this is the\nday when most locations will likely see their lowest relative \nhumidities. Slight improvement on Sunday is expected with \nregards to fire weather concerns as some modest moisture\nadvection occurs. Light winds through the weekend should\nminimize fire spread, however.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The stretch of quiet and dry weather looks continue into \nnext week as the region remains under the influence of high \npressure. The ridge axis will shift slightly eastward late Sunday \ninto Monday, allowing for some light southwest flow and warmer \ntemperatures. Daytime high temperatures on Monday look to warm into \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s. A weak cold front looks to move through \nlate Monday, but it will be quite moisture starved with no \nprecipitation expected from this feature. Some cooler air will move \nin behind the front, with high temperatures on Tuesday expected to \nbe in the mid 40s to low 50s. At this time, it looks like the dry \nstretch will continue through much of next week. Despite the \nprolonged stretch of dryness, winds look to remain light enough to \nlimit any fore weather concerns at this time.",
